/*  The double-hash conditionals belong to emitter bodies, where `##` distinguishes a Beguile
    conditional from the raw I6 around it. In Beguile source there is no I6 to distinguish from and
    `#if` is the conditional — 7.4 says the double-hash forms "are not valid in ordinary Beguile
    source", and appendix B lists them with scope "emitter bodies".

    They were dispatched here anyway, which left the two scopes with exactly inverted vocabularies:
    `##ifdef` worked in source and was an error in an emitter body ("use '##if <expr>'"), while
    `##if` worked in an emitter body and was "Unrecognized statement" in source. Whichever you
    learned first was the wrong one for the other place.  */
// EXPECT_ERROR: '##ifdef' is not a Beguile directive
#beguilerSettings { target = z5; informName = "none"; }
#define FEATURE
void main(){
##ifdef FEATURE
    print("A");
##endif
}
